2 | orchards | Almost every farm of any size in Gettysburg in 1863 had an orchard, usually in proportion to the farmstead (park historians) | - | 1863 | stated | The newsletter says that according to park historians almost every farm of any size in 1863 Gettysburg had an orchard, usually of a size in proportion to the farmstead | =almost every farm of any size in 1863 Gettysburg had an orchard, usually of a size in proportion to the farmstead. ~R
2 | orchards | The roles of the orchards in the battle: cover from observation or fire, concealment during movement, obstruction of observation or of fields of fire, places to rest or seek medical assistance | - | 1863-07 | stated | The newsletter says the orchards played many roles during the battle: cover from observation or from fire for troops and batteries, concealment during movement, obstructions to observation or clear fields of fire, and places to gather to rest or seek medical assistance | =The orchards played many roles during the battle- cover from observation or from fire for both troops and artillery batteries; concealment during movement; obstructions to observation or clear fields of fire; places to gather to rest or seek medical assistance. ~R
2 | orchards | Four historic orchards replanted in April 2009: the 26-acre McMillan orchard, the orchards of the Timbers farm, the Klingel farm and the Joseph Spangler farm at East Cavalry Battlefield | - | 2009 replanting (modern) | stated | The newsletter says that in early April the park replanted four more historic orchards, among them the 26 acre McMillan Orchard, the largest in the park, and those at the Timbers Farm, the Klingel farm and the Joseph Spangler farm at East Cavalry Battlefield | =In early April, the park replanted four more historic orchards in major battle action areas on the battlefield so visitors can better understand the fighting and see the battlefield through the eyes of the soldiers fighting in 1863. Hively Landscapes of Dover, Pa., planted the trees - including the 26 acre McMillan Orchard which is the largest orchard in the park. Also included were the orchards at the Timbers Farm, Klingel farm and at the Joseph Spangler farm at East Cavalry Battlefield. ~R
2 | orchards | The replanted orchards of 2009 use modern apple varieties: Williams Pride, Pristine, Gold Rush, Liberty, Enterprise and Freedom | - | 2009 replanting (modern cultivars; not the varieties of 1863) | stated | The newsletter says the new orchard trees are hardy varieties of apple named Williams Pride, Pristine, Gold Rush, Liberty, Enterprise and Freedom | =The new orchard trees are hardy varieties of apple: Williams Pride, Pristine, Gold Rush, Liberty, Enterprise, and Freedom. ~R
2 | orchards | Since 2000 the park has replanted 110 acres at 36 orchard sites, with a goal of 160 acres of orchards in the major battle action areas | - | ~2009 (the totals of the replanting programme; the 160 acres is a goal, not an 1863 total) | stated | The newsletter says that since 2000 the park has replanted 110 acres at 36 historic orchard sites and that the goal is to replant a total of 160 acres of orchards throughout the major battle action areas | =Since 2000, the park has replanted 110 acres at 36 historic orchard sites. The goal is to replant a total of 160 acres of orchards throughout the major battle action areas of the battlefield. ~R
3 | woodlots | Next rehabilitation work of 2009: removal of non-historic trees on the McAllister farm, near Spangler's Spring and on 16 acres west of the McMillan farm; health cuts in historic woodlots along Herr's Ridge and at the McPherson and Wills woodlots | - | ~2009 (planned modern work) | stated | The newsletter says the next projects were the continued removal of nonhistoric trees on the McAllister farm, near Spangler's Spring and on 16 acres on the west side of the historic McMillan farm, and health cuts in the historic woodlots along Herr's Ridge and at the McPherson and Wills woodlots | =continuing removal of nonhistoric trees on the McAllister farm, near Spangler's Spring and on 16 acres on the west side of the historic McMillan farm. Health cuts will continue in historic woodlots along Herr's Ridge and at the McPherson/Wills woodlots. ~R

