@ NPS-NEWSLETTER-V17N1
1 | buildings | Klingel house: a log house on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge north of the Peach Orchard; on July 2 Carr's brigade knocked out the chinking of its exposed log walls to fire from it; on July 3 it screened Confederate artillery in the field just west | bld-klingle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The newsletter says the Klingel house on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge north of the Peach Orchard was in contested ground, that on July 2 men of Carr's Union brigade fortified themselves within it, knocking out the chinking in the exposed log walls to fire, and that on July 3 the building screened Confederate artillery in the field just west of it | =The Klingel house's location on the Emmitsburg Road Ridge north of the Peach Orchard placed it in contested territory during the battle of Gettysburg. On July 2, men of Carr's Union brigade fortified themselves within the house during Confederate attacks, knocking out the chinking in the exposed log walls in order to fire against enemy infantry. On July 3, the building was used to shield and screen Confederate artillery in the field just west of the house preparatory to and during the cannonade prior to the Pickett-Pettigrew Charge. ~R

